<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francesco Foscari</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1373–<a href="/years/1457">1457</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francesco Foscari was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doge-of-venice">Doge of Venice</a> from 1423 to 1457, the longest reign of any doge. He led the republic into long wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> that won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> and Bergamo and carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>'s mainland frontier to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adda">Adda</a>. In 1457, after the exile of his son Jacopo, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a> forced him to abdicate, and he died a week later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francesco-foscari.jpg" alt="Francesco Foscari" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:(Venice)_Portrait_of_the_Venecian_doge_Francesco_Foscari_by_Lazzaro_Bastiani_-_Correr_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francesco Foscari was born on 19 June 1373, the eldest son of Nicolò Foscari and Cateruzia Michiel. The Foscari were a family of only moderate importance who had secured a hereditary place in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-council-of-venice">Great Council of Venice</a> after the Serrata, the closing of the council, and rose in prominence during the fourteenth century.</p><p>His father became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a>. Francesco served the republic as ambassador, president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chihalgani">Council of Forty</a>, member of the Council of Ten, inquisitor, Procurator of St Mark's and avogador de comùn before his election as doge in 1423, in which he defeated the other candidate, Pietro Loredan. He married twice: first Maria Priuli, then in 1415 Marina Nani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>His dogeship lasted 34 years, 6 months and 8 days, the longest in Venetian history, and coincided with the early Italian Renaissance, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> grew in prominence in art, humanism and diplomacy. His task was to lead the republic through long wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, governed by the Visconti, who sought to dominate northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>The wars marked a critical phase in the republic's expansion onto the mainland, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domini-di-terraferma">Domini di Terraferma</a>. They were very costly to a city whose wealth and power lay at sea. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> abandoned its ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> during his leadership and was eventually overcome by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>'s forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-sforza">Francesco Sforza</a>, who soon made peace with Florence and left Venice alone. The humanist senator Bernardo Giustiniani justified the mainland wars in Foscari's funeral oration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1445 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-ten">Council of Ten</a> tried his only surviving son Jacopo for bribery and corruption and exiled him. Two further trials followed, in 1450 and 1456; in the second Jacopo confessed that he had appealed for help to Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> and to the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, both enemies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. He was imprisoned on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and died there soon after. The news led Foscari to withdraw from his duties, and in October 1457 the Council of Ten forced him to abdicate. He died within days, and public outcry over this brought him a state funeral.</p><p>He was painted in profile by Lazzaro Bastiani and commissioned a bronze plaquette from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a>. A figure of him kneeling before St Mark stood above the portal of the Doge's Palace until the revolutionary government dismantled it in 1797, and his monument by Antonio Bregno and his brother Paolo stands in Santa Maria dei Frari. Lord Byron's play The Two Foscari of 1821 was the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Giuseppe Verdi</a>'s opera I due Foscari, first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 3 November 1844.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francesco Foscari” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1427" aria-label="Venice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4843 561 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francesco Foscari"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5293 831h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5293 831h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.44,12.33&amp;year=1427">What was here in 1427? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
